<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho Ramírez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1042–1094</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sancho V of Navarre, Sancho I of Aragon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho Ramírez was King of Aragon from 1063 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>) from 1076 until his death in 1094, the son of Ramiro I. He placed Aragon under papal protection, introduced the Roman liturgy and pushed the frontier south against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He died at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, which his son Peter I took in 1096.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez.jpg" alt="Sancho Ramírez" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sancho Ramírez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mancuso_de_Sancho_Ram%C3%ADrez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
